<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42692160</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>04</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1542-7714</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Sep</Month><Day>03</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Clinical gastroenterology and hepatology : the official clinical practice journal of the American Gastroenterological Association</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Clin Gastroenterol Hepatol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>STARS Phase 3 Trial: Once-Weekly Apraglutide Reduces Parenteral Support in Short Bowel Syndrome-Intestinal Failure.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1542-3565(26)00649-X</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cgh.2026.08.026</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND &amp; AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Patients with short bowel syndrome with intestinal failure (SBS-IF) depend on parenteral support (PS) to meet nutritional and fluid requirements. Apraglutide, a once-weekly, long-acting glucagon-like peptide-2 analog, promotes intestinal adaptation and may reduce PS dependence. The phase 3 STARS trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of apraglutide vs placebo in SBS-IF.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this global, phase 3, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, eligible patients were randomized 2:1 to once-weekly subcutaneous apraglutide or placebo, stratified by remnant intestinal anatomy (stoma or colon-in-continuity [CIC]). The primary end point was relative change from baseline in weekly PS volume at week 24 in the overall population. Key secondary end points included relative PS volume change in the stoma subgroup, days off PS (overall and CIC), and enteral autonomy (CIC). A safety analysis was conducted.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Overall, 163 patients (80 stoma, 83 CIC) were randomized and dosed. Apraglutide significantly reduced weekly PS volume from baseline vs placebo at week 24 in the overall population (mean relative change, -25.5% vs -12.5%; P = .001) and stoma subgroup (-25.6% vs -7.8%; P &lt; .001). A higher proportion of patients receiving apraglutide vs placebo achieved a reduction of &#x2265;1 day/week of PS at week 24 (43.0% vs 27.5%; P = .040). At week 48 (CIC), in the apraglutide vs placebo arms, the proportions of patients achieving a reduction of &#x2265;1 day/week of PS and enteral autonomy were 51.8% vs 44.4% (P = .348) and 12.5% vs 7.4% (nominal P = .387), respectively. Apraglutide was well-tolerated with a favorable safety profile.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Once-weekly subcutaneous apraglutide significantly reduced PS requirements in patients with SBS-IF with a well-tolerated safety profile.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CLINICALTRIALS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">gov, Number: NCT04627025. ClinicalTrialsRegister.eu, Number: 2020-001202-32.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Joly</LastName><ForeName>Francisca</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Gastroenterology, Hepatology &amp; Nutrition, IBD and Nutrition Support, H&#xf4;pital Beaujon, Clichy, UMR 1149, University of Paris, Paris, France. Electronic address: francisca.joly@aphp.fr.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Vanuytsel</LastName><ForeName>Tim</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Leuven Intestinal Failure and Transplantation (LIFT), University Hospitals Leuven, Leuven, Belgium.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kirby</LastName><ForeName>Donald F</ForeName><Initials>DF